= DIKW pyramid — Simple =

The '''DIKW pyramid''' is a common way of separating '''data''', '''information''', '''knowledge''', and '''wisdom'''. It is usually drawn with data at the bottom and wisdom at the top.

A simple example is:

* '''Data:''' a sensor records 31 °C.
* '''Information:''' the reading is identified as the temperature of a particular room at a particular time.
* '''Knowledge:''' evidence and experience support an explanation of why the room is overheating.
* '''Wisdom:''' a decision takes account of goals, uncertainty, consequences, and values.

The example is useful for explaining different roles, but the DIKW pyramid is **not a scientific law** saying that data automatically become knowledge or wisdom.

== Where the idea came from ==

Several earlier writers discussed hierarchies involving information, knowledge, and wisdom. A particularly influential version was Russell Ackoff's 1989 article “From Data to Wisdom.” Ackoff actually used five levels—data, information, knowledge, understanding, and wisdom—not the exact four-level pyramid often shown today.<ref name="ackoff">[https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Rob-Keller/post/Original_paper_of_From_data_to_wisdom_by_Ackoff_1989/attachment/63f67d8997e2867d5081d0de/AS%3A11431281121841684%401677098376991/download/Ackoff89.pdf Ackoff, R. L. (1989), “From Data to Wisdom.”]</ref>

Later books, diagrams, and management frameworks produced many variations. Jennifer Rowley's review found that the model was widely used but that authors did not agree on exact definitions or on how one level becomes another.<ref name="rowley">[https://doi.org/10.1177/0165551506070706 Rowley, J. (2007), “The wisdom hierarchy: representations of the DIKW hierarchy.”]</ref>

== Why the hierarchy is controversial ==

Real knowledge creation is usually not a one-way staircase.

Before collecting data, researchers already need concepts, questions, instruments, and background knowledge. Data can then change what researchers believe. New [[Evidence]] can support or undermine a claim. Decisions about what to do also depend on goals and values.

So a more realistic process often contains loops:

<pre>
questions → measurements → data → interpretation
    ↑                            ↓
knowledge ← evidence ← testing ← claims
    ↓
new questions and decisions
</pre>

The same record can also play different roles in different contexts. A number stored in a file might be uninterpreted data for one person but useful information for someone who knows its units and source.

== Information is not just one thing ==

The word [[Information]] has several disciplinary meanings. In ordinary DIKW diagrams, information often means data given context or meaning. In [[Information theory]], however, information is described using formal probability and communication measures.

These are not the same definition. A message can contain many bits in the Shannon sense without being true, useful, or meaningful.

== Knowledge needs justification ==

Processing data does not automatically create reliable [[Knowledge]]. A claim may require good measurements, appropriate methods, independent evidence, testing, uncertainty estimates, and correction of errors.

Because of this problem, some researchers have proposed modified models that place '''evidence''' between information and knowledge.<ref name="diek">[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6435353/ “Data, Information, Evidence, and Knowledge” (2019).]</ref>

== Wisdom is especially difficult to define ==

Wisdom is usually associated with good judgment: deciding what should be done, considering consequences, recognizing uncertainty, and weighing values.

That is different from simply having more data or producing more accurate predictions. A computer system can process enormous datasets without that fact alone establishing wisdom.

== Main criticism ==

Martin Frické argued that the DIKW hierarchy can be misleading because it suggests an overly simple rise from data to knowledge and relies on weak assumptions about how knowledge is produced.<ref name="fricke">[https://doi.org/10.1177/0165551508094050 Frické, M. (2009), “The knowledge pyramid: a critique of the DIKW hierarchy.”]</ref>

The best use of DIKW is therefore as a **discussion tool**: it can help ask what is being recorded, what context is being added, what is actually justified, and how decisions are being made. It should not be treated as proof that those stages exist as a universal hierarchy.

